# Directed Line Edits

Do only what they named. No other lines, files, or cleanup.

| They say | You do |
| --- | --- |
| Change / fix / replace | Change only that part |
| Insert | Insert at the mark. Delete nothing |
| Append | Append after the mark. Delete nothing |
| Unclear | Ask. Do not guess |

## Absolute anchor

The **first word** of their proposed change is the mark.

1. Find that word on the clicked stretch.
2. Edit from there only.
3. Change **nothing** before it. Every character before the mark stays exact.
4. Do not jump to a different paragraph, sentence, or “related” beat.

## Order lock

Keep their sentence and clause order exactly. Do not move “while,” luck lines, or any other clause to where you think it reads better. Do not glue their words onto a later verb. Do not rewrite for grammar taste.

## Exact words

- Paste their words exact (fix clear typos; capitalize sentence starts).
- No synonym swaps. No style rewrites. No slang cleanup on their pasted lines.
- Do not delete an opening sentence they did not mark.
- Do not invent names, places, numbers, or fills. Ask, or keep their wording.
- Do not graft leftover Source clauses onto their beat unless they kept them in the prompt.

## Locates

`word?`, `...`, and short locate lines are not story text. Use them to find the match. Do not paste them into the story.

Where: click first; else first-word anchor / locate / `...`. Same first word as an existing sentence → replace from that sentence. Undo = wrong; ask if still unclear. Do not re-guess.
